**Goal** — Add pure magic-byte recognition and header dimensions for PNG, GIF, JPEG and WebP in src/chat/images/image-sniff.ts.

**Motivation** — The user wants image input in `nax chat` for parity with Claude Code and Codex (user, 2026-10-31).

**Approach** — `image-sniff.ts` holds `ImageFormat`, `SniffedImage` and `sniffImage`; `image-file.ts` holds the rest and imports `./image-sniff`; `index.ts` re-exports both. Code outside the folder (`@/chat` itself, `mention-expand.ts`) imports through the `index.ts` barrel.

`sniffImage` decides the format from magic bytes only, never from a file name, and reads dimensions from the header:

| Format | Magic | Width / height |
|---|---|---|
| PNG | bytes 0-7 `89 50 4E 47 0D 0A 1A 0A` | big-endian u32 at offsets 16 and 20 (IHDR) |
| GIF | ASCII `GIF87a` or `GIF89a` at 0 | little-endian u16 at offsets 6 and 8 |
| JPEG | bytes 0-2 `FF D8 FF` | walk the markers from offset 2: skip `FF` fill bytes; the stand-alone markers `01` and `D0`-`D8` have no length; every other marker is followed by a big-endian u16 segment length (which includes those two bytes) that is skipped. The first SOF marker (`C0`-`CF` except `C4`, `C8`, `CC`) holds height then width as big-endian u16 at offsets 5 and 7 from its `FF`. Reaching SOS (`DA`), EOI (`D9`) or the end of the bytes first gives no dimensions |
| WebP | ASCII `RIFF` at 0 and `WEBP` at 8 | chunk at 12: `VP8 ` = little-endian u16 at 26 and 28, each `& 0x3FFF`; `VP8L` = from the 4 bytes at 21, width `1 + (b0 | (b1 & 0x3F) << 8)`, height `1 + (b1 >> 6 | b2 << 2 | (b3 & 0x0F) << 10)`; `VP8X` = `1 +` little-endian u24 at 24 and 27 |

A recognised format whose header is too short or malformed for its dimensions is returned without `width` and `height`. Anything else is `undefined`.

**Scope** — In: the pure sniffer and its tests. Out: byte-to-attachment validation, labels and chat-barrel exports (US-002), clipboard I/O (US-003), and resizing, cropping or re-encoding images.

**Interface**
```ts
export type ImageFormat = "png" | "jpeg" | "gif" | "webp";
export interface SniffedImage {
  readonly format: ImageFormat;
  readonly mimeType: string; // "image/png" | "image/jpeg" | "image/gif" | "image/webp"
  readonly width?: number;
  readonly height?: number;
}
export function sniffImage(bytes: Uint8Array): SniffedImage | undefined;
```

**Goal** — Add image clipboard reads with platform-specific commands, bounded subprocess lifetime and injectable effects.

**Motivation** — The clipboard module is write-only (`packages/nax/src/tui/chat/clipboard.ts`), ctrl+v is ignored by the line editor (`line-editor.ts` `ctrlResult`).

**Approach**
| Platform | Command | Image when |
|---|---|---|
| `darwin` | `["osascript", "-e", "the clipboard as «class PNGf»"]` | exit 0 and stdout matches `«data PNGf([0-9A-Fa-f]+)»`; the hex digits decode to the bytes |
| `linux`, `WAYLAND_DISPLAY` non-empty | `["wl-paste", "--no-newline", "--type", "image/png"]` | exit 0 and stdout non-empty; stdout is the bytes |
| `linux` otherwise | `["xclip", "-selection", "clipboard", "-t", "image/png", "-o"]` | exit 0 and stdout non-empty |
| anything else | none | never: `unsupported`, nothing is run |

A non-zero exit, or an exit 0 with no image, is `none`. `missing` (the command is not installed) is `failed` with reason `<argv[0]> is not installed`; `timed-out` is `failed` with `clipboard read timed out`; a `run` rejection is `failed` with the error's message. `run` is always called with `CLIPBOARD_READ_TIMEOUT_MS`. The default `run` spawns the command with stdout piped and stderr ignored, kills it when the timeout passes, and resolves `missing` when the spawn fails because the executable does not exist. Its timer may be a cancelled `setTimeout` (the rule's mid-flight cancel exception; comment the call site).

**Scope** — In: clipboard-image.ts including its default Bun.spawn runner, typed results, timeout cleanup, unit command selection and real runner integration behavior. Out: Ctrl+v key wiring/notices (US-010), /image dispatch (US-011), clipboard text pasting and clipboard images on non-macOS/non-Linux platforms. Leave clipboard copy/OSC52 behavior unchanged.

**Interface**
```ts
export type ClipboardImage =
  | { readonly kind: "image"; readonly bytes: Uint8Array }
  | { readonly kind: "none" }
  | { readonly kind: "unsupported" }
  | { readonly kind: "failed"; readonly reason: string };
export type ClipboardRun =
  | { readonly kind: "exited"; readonly exitCode: number; readonly stdout: Uint8Array }
  | { readonly kind: "missing" }
  | { readonly kind: "timed-out" };
export const CLIPBOARD_READ_TIMEOUT_MS = 2_000;
export const _clipboardImageDeps: {
  platform: NodeJS.Platform;
  env: () => Record<string, string | undefined>;
  run: (argv: readonly string[], timeoutMs: number) => Promise<ClipboardRun>;
};
export function readClipboardImage(): Promise<ClipboardImage>;
```

**Goal** — Add immutable numbered image-draft operations and derive ordered, consistently renumbered attachments from surviving placeholders.

**Motivation** — Feature 1 made images travel from `AgentSession.send` to the model and to disk, but nothing in chat can create one.

**Approach** — Attachments live in the chat screen, next to the input draft, not in it: `InputDraft` (`ChatInput.tsx`) is unchanged and a separate pure `ImageDraft` holds the numbered attachments. The line editor stays pure. ChatApp does the I/O through a new hook module, `use-image-attach.ts`, which owns the `ImageDraft` and the deps seam for the clipboard and file reads.

Image numbers: every attachment takes the draft's next number, starting at 1. On send, only images whose placeholder `[Image #N]` is still in the text travel, ordered by the first appearance of their placeholder, and the placeholders are renumbered `1..k` in that order (every occurrence of a number is rewritten). So "image 1" in the text the model sees is always the first image part. Images from `@` mentions come after the placeholder images, in mention order, and carry no placeholder: the mention envelope record names their path. (**Claude** call: renumbering is not in the design doc; it keeps the transcript rows, replay and the model's view consistent when a placeholder was deleted.)

`attachToDraft` counts the draft images whose placeholder is in `editor.text` ("present"). It refuses with `at most 10 images per message` when 10 (`MAX_IMAGES_PER_MESSAGE`) are present, then with `images in one message must total at most 20 MB` when the present images' `data.length` plus the new one's is over `MAX_REQUEST_IMAGE_BASE64_CHARS`. Otherwise it inserts `imagePlaceholder(draft.next)` at `editor.cursor` (a code-point index, as the line editor uses), puts the cursor after it, appends `{ number: draft.next, image }` and increments `next`. It never changes its inputs.

`imagesForSend` finds every `[Image #<digits>]` in `text` whose number is a draft image, orders those images by first appearance, renumbers them `1..k` in that order, rewrites every occurrence of each known placeholder to its new number, and leaves any other `[Image #N]` text as typed. It returns the rewritten text and the images in that order.

**Scope** — In: image-draft.ts and pure immutability, count/size, Unicode cursor and renumbering behavior. Out: hook ownership/effects (US-010), capability/mention preparation (US-012), resets and history wiring (US-014).

**Interface**
```ts
import type { ImageAttachment } from "@nathapp/nax-agent";
import type { EditorState } from "./line-editor";

export interface DraftImage { readonly number: number; readonly image: ImageAttachment }
export interface ImageDraft { readonly next: number; readonly images: readonly DraftImage[] }
export const EMPTY_IMAGE_DRAFT: ImageDraft; // { next: 1, images: [] }
export function imagePlaceholder(number: number): string; // "[Image #<number>]"
export function attachToDraft(draft: ImageDraft, editor: EditorState, image: ImageAttachment):
  | { readonly draft: ImageDraft; readonly editor: EditorState }
  | { readonly refusal: string };
export function imagesForSend(draft: ImageDraft, text: string): { readonly text: string; readonly images: readonly ImageAttachment[] };
```

**Goal** — Pass nonempty image inputs through the chat host and turn runner and expose fail-safe open-session capability lookup.

**Motivation** — `ChatHost.send(message, onEvent, options)` and `runChatTurn` call `session.send(message)` with text only.

**Approach** —
- `ChatSendOptions` gains `images?: readonly ImageAttachment[]`; `RunChatTurnOptions` gains the same. `ChatHostImpl.send` passes `options.images` to `runChatTurn`, which calls `handle.session.send(message, { images })` when `images` is non-empty and `handle.session.send(message)` (one argument) otherwise.
- `ChatHost` gains optional `acceptsImages?(): Promise<boolean>` (optional so the hand-built fake hosts in tests still typecheck). The logic lives in a new `packages/nax/src/chat/session-images.ts`: `sessionAcceptsImages(session: AgentSession | undefined): Promise<boolean>` resolves `false` for no session, `false` when the session has no `acceptsImages`, `false` when it rejects, and otherwise its answer. `ChatHostImpl.acceptsImages()` is a one-line delegation `sessionAcceptsImages(this.handle?.session)`, so it never throws when no session is open. ChatApp treats a host without the method, or a rejection, as `false`.

In this checkout the spec's ChatHostImpl is the unexported NativeChatHost; implement there without renaming or adding a parallel host. `chat-host.ts` is 586 lines and `check:file-sizes` (600-line limit for `.ts` sources, no baseline entry for it) fails it at 601, so its additions are limited to the `ImageAttachment` import, the two optional members, the one-line `acceptsImages` delegation and the `images` pass-through. Add the helper import needed by that delegation. Keep ledger, asks, cancellation, nested cost and metadata semantics unchanged.

**Scope** — In: src/chat/chat-host.ts, turn-runner.ts, new session-images.ts and tests through createChatHost/runChatTurn. Out: ACP wrapper image propagation (US-008), image preparation (US-012), screen capability handling (US-013), and reducing chat-host.ts below the source limit by unrelated refactoring.

**Interface**
```ts
// ChatSendOptions and RunChatTurnOptions gain:
readonly images?: readonly ImageAttachment[];
// ChatHost gains:
acceptsImages?(): Promise<boolean>;
export function sessionAcceptsImages(session: AgentSession | undefined): Promise<boolean>;
```

**Goal** — Preserve image input and capability through ACP recording and represent earlier images as bounded text notes on brain handoff.

**Motivation** — The ACP recorder (`chat/acp/acp-recorder.ts`) wraps `send(message)` without an input and without `acceptsImages`.

**Approach** —
- `recordAcpTurns` (`acp-recorder.ts`): `send(message, input?)` passes `input` through as the wrapped `send`'s second argument only when `input?.images` is non-empty (the handoff prefix rule is unchanged), and records the turn with `acpTurnMessages({ message, images, events, acp })`. The wrapper gains a getter `acceptsImages` that returns the wrapped session's `acceptsImages` bound to it, or `undefined` when it has none.
- `acpTurnMessages` (`acp-history.ts`) takes optional `images`; the recorded user message is `{ role: "user", content: message, images }` when non-empty, unchanged otherwise. The file store writes them to its image folder (feature 1).
- Handoff (`acp-handoff-entries.ts`): a user message's entry text is its `content` followed by one line per image, in order, `[image <n>: <imageFormatOf(mimeType)> <width>×<height>, <source>; not carried across the switch]` (`<n>` from 1; ` <width>×<height>` only when both are present; `, <source>` only when present). Lines are joined with `\n`, with no leading `\n` when `content` is empty. The entry cap applies to the joined text.

Preserve the synchronous wrapped send turn-slot claim, original (unprefixed) message recording, pending-handoff consumption only after successful agent work and turn_end, tracked appends and close draining. The entry cap retains existing UTF-16 truncation plus `…`. Import imageFormatOf through the images barrel. Existing exceptions from the wrapped send propagate unchanged; the wrapper does not convert transport failures into attachment notices.

**Scope** — In: src/chat/acp/acp-recorder.ts, acp-history.ts, acp-handoff-entries.ts and image recorder/handoff tests. Out: host plumbing (US-007), replay rows (US-009), upstream image-store implementation, and re-sending earlier images when switching brains: the handoff carries a text note per image only.

**Interface**
```ts
// Returned AgentSession:
send(message: string, input?: SendInput): AsyncIterable<SessionEvent>;
// Getter returns wrapped session.acceptsImages bound to the wrapped session, or undefined.
// acpTurnMessages input gains:
readonly images?: readonly ImageAttachment[];
```

**Goal** — Compose surviving placeholder images and mentioned images into one validated send payload with a single fail-safe capability decision.

**Motivation** — `ChatHost.send(message, onEvent, options)` and `runChatTurn` call `session.send(message)` with text only.

**Approach** — Steps, in order:

1. `imagesForSend(draft, text)` gives the renumbered text and the placeholder images.
2. With placeholder images, `acceptsImages()` false → refuse `<model> cannot read images; switch model (/model) or remove the image`.
3. When `needsMentionExpansion(renumbered)`: `expand(renumbered, where)` when the brain does not read images, and `expand(renumbered, { ...where, images: true })` when it does (the answer is step 2's, or one `acceptsImages()` call when step 2 did not ask), so a text-only brain's expander call keeps today's exact arguments; the message is the expanded one and the images are the placeholder images followed by `expanded.images`. Otherwise the message is the renumbered text.
4. `validateImageAttachments(images)` (`@nathapp/nax-agent`) returning a reason → refuse `images not sent: <reason>`.
5. `{ kind: "send", message, images }`.

A rejected `acceptsImages()` counts as `false`. A mentioned image on such a brain degrades to today's path-only record instead of refusing the send, so `@` mentions on a text-only model behave as before. Placeholder images precede mentioned images and unknown placeholder text remains text, using imagesForSend.

**Scope** — In: image-send.ts typed preparation and composition, combined image limits, exact mention-expander argument compatibility, one capability lookup per send that needs it. Out: host/session implementation (US-007/008), screen submit/refusal restoration/payload (US-013), draft reset/transcript (US-014), and per-image capability probing beyond acceptsImages.

**Interface**
```ts
export interface ImageSendInput {
  readonly text: string;            // the submitted text
  readonly draft: ImageDraft;
  readonly where: MentionWhere;
  readonly expand: MentionExpander;  // _chatAppDeps.expandMentions
  readonly acceptsImages: () => Promise<boolean>;
  readonly model: string;           // host.current().model
}
export type ImageSendResult =
  | { readonly kind: "send"; readonly message: string; readonly images: readonly ImageAttachment[] }
  | { readonly kind: "refuse"; readonly notice: string };
export function prepareImageSend(input: ImageSendInput): Promise<ImageSendResult>;
```

**Goal** — Connect ChatApp submit to image preparation and host image payloads, restoring original text and attachments on every preparation refusal.

**Motivation** — `ChatHost.send(message, onEvent, options)` and `runChatTurn` call `session.send(message)` with text only, and the ACP recorder (`chat/acp/acp-recorder.ts`) wraps `send(message)` without an input and without `acceptsImages`.

**Approach** — ChatApp's plain-message `submit` keeps today's exact path when the draft has no present placeholder image and the text needs no mention expansion. Otherwise it sends through `prepareImageSend`. On `refuse` it appends the notice, writes the submitted text back into the prompt (cursor at the end, through the input handle) and keeps the `ImageDraft`, so return sends it again. On `send` it calls `host.send(message, onEvent, { title, images })` when `images` is non-empty and today's call otherwise. Every plain message that reaches `host.send` (on either path) resets the `ImageDraft` to `EMPTY_IMAGE_DRAFT`, so a draft whose placeholders were all deleted does not keep its numbers. A rejected `acceptsImages()` counts as `false`. A submitted line that starts with `/` while a draft image is present is refused before the command runs, with `images can only be sent with a plain message`, and the line goes back into the prompt. Up-arrow history keeps text only: a recalled `[Image #N]` with no draft image behind it is plain text.

`ChatHost` gains optional `acceptsImages?(): Promise<boolean>` (optional so the hand-built fake hosts in tests still typecheck). ChatApp treats a host without the method, or a rejection, as `false`. Capability: `ChatHost.acceptsImages?()` asks the open session (`session.acceptsImages?.()`, from feature 1). A brain that cannot read images refuses a send that has placeholder images, keeps the draft and says why. A mentioned image on such a brain degrades to today's path-only record instead of refusing the send, so `@` mentions on a text-only model behave as before. (**Claude** call.)

Preserve the typed text as title/history, existing busy guards, event callback, mention expansion seam and title-only options for sends without images. Restore using ChatInputHandle.edit because ChatInput.submit has already emptied its editor; cursor is a code-point end index. Slash-image refusal occurs before command effects, including model switches. Use the current model from host.current().model. New algorithms remain in image-send/use-image-attach; ChatApp takes wiring only.

**Scope** — In: ChatApp plain-message preparation selection, fail-safe host capability accessor, slash-line refusal, refusal text/draft restoration, nonempty host images options and mounted screen tests. Out: preparing algorithms (US-012), hook effects (US-010), ACP recording (US-008), transcript image rows and every-plain-send reset/history verification (US-014 implements that after-send part of the quoted design). Images with slash commands, skills or `!` shell lines remain out of scope.

**Interface**
```ts
// Use upstream contracts without changing them:
prepareImageSend(input: ImageSendInput): Promise<ImageSendResult>;
host.send(message, onEvent, { title, images });
host.acceptsImages?(): Promise<boolean>;
```
